{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>Whom HaShem knew face to face</b> — that His heart was familiar with him and He would speak to him at every moment he desired, as in what is said, \"Now I will go up to HaShem\" (Exodus 32:30), \"Stand and I will hear what HaShem will command you\" (Numbers 9:8) — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Deuteronomy 34:10). But it is not correct, for then it should say אֲשֶׁר יָדַע אֶת הַשֵּׁם פָּנִים אֵל פָּנִים (\"whom he knew HaShem face to face\"). Rather, it is from the language of \"and I will know you by name\" (אֲוֺדָעֲךָ בְּשֵׁם) (Exodus 33:17), and \"you said, 'I have known you by name'\" (יְדַעְתִּיךָ בְּשֵׁם) (ibid. v. 12). He says that no prophet has arisen again in Israel like Moses, whom HaShem elevated to be before Him face to face in speech. And likewise, \"What is man that You should know him\" (מָה אָדָם וַתֵּדָעֵהוּ) (Psalms 144:3) — that You do him honor to set Your regard upon him, as in \"What is man that You should magnify him\" (מָה אֱנוֹשׁ כִּי תְגַדְּלֶנּוּ) (Job 7:17). And it is possible that because two people who meet face to face become known to one another through that very sighting, he said <b>HaShem knew him face to face</b> — for He did not wish to mention that Moses knew Him, out of the respect due from above. And do not wonder at the fact that in praise of Moses He said \"whom HaShem knew face to face,\" while of all Israel it is likewise said (Deuteronomy 5:4) \"face to face HaShem spoke with you\" — for there He specified <b>from the midst of the fire</b>, to say that they heard His voice from the midst of the fire alone and did not see the Face. And so He explained (Deuteronomy 4:36), \"and on earth He showed you His great fire, and you heard His words from the midst of the fire.\" And by way of truth, that HaShem knew him face to face to cleave to Him at the time when the spirit would come upon Moses our Master — and so it was at the time of the giving of the Torah; but they grasped nothing except a voice from the midst of the fire. And behold, this explains that all the prophecies of Moses were like the day of the giving of the Torah, while the rest of the prophets had upon them the hand of HaShem. Understand this."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 34, :notes {0 "I read לִהְיוֹת אֵלָיו as 'to be before Him,' preserving the construct chain with פָּנִים אֵל פָּנִים. The phrase כִּי הָיָה לִבּוֹ גַּס בּוֹ I read as 'His heart was familiar with him' (גַּס as 'thick/close'), which is the sense Rashi's reading requires; the word is unusual and could conceivably be a copyist's variant."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:00:10.496182608Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}